<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">White Sea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The White Sea is an inlet of the Barents Sea on the north-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, enclosed by the Kola Peninsula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>. Its coasts formed part of Novgorod's northern lands and were settled by Pomors. English merchants reached it in 1553, opening a trade route to Muscovy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Arkhangelsk</a> was Russia's main seaport until the founding of St Petersburg. The Solovetsky Monastery stands on its islands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-sea.jpg" alt="White Sea" width="320" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NormanEinstein · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Sea_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By his death the state stretched from the <b>White Sea</b> to the steppe frontier south of the Oka and from Smolensk towards the Urals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Through tribute collected from Finnic and Ugric peoples and through trading expeditions, Novgorodians gathered furs, above all squirrel pelts, as well as wax, honey and walrus ivory from lands stretching to the <b>White Sea</b>, the Pechora and beyond the Urals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade grew steadily: the English navigator Richard Chancellor reached the <b>White Sea</b> in 1553, the Muscovy Company was chartered in London in 1555, and Arkhangelsk, founded in 1584, became the main port for trade with western Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Along the north Norwegian coast the Iron Age brought expanding Norse settlement and taxation by Norse chieftains; the nature of that relationship is debated, and the Sámi may have allied with the chieftains for protection against enemies from the <b>White Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sami">Sámi · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The government controlled only the area around Uhtua in the <b>White Sea</b> Karelia region.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-uhtua">Republic of Uhtua</a></li></ul></section>
